## This turn's refactoring: `AnsiLog` deduplication (`common` module)

`AnsiLog` had two blocks of near-identical copy-pasted methods. Both are now backed by small private helpers, with the public API fully unchanged:

**1. The 8 color methods** (`black`/`red`/`green`/`yellow`/`blue`/`magenta`/`cyan`/`white`) each had an identical 7-line `if (enableColor) … else …` body differing only in the color constant. They now delegate to one helper:

```java
private static String color(String msg, int colorCode) {
    return enableColor ? colorStr(msg, colorCode) : msg;
}
public static String red(String msg) { return color(msg, RED); }   // ×8
```

**2. The 5 log-level methods** (`trace`/`debug`/`info`/`warn`/`error`) — the `(String msg)` and `(Throwable)` variants each repeated the same `canLog` guard and color-prefix selection. They now delegate to two helpers:

```java
private static void log(Level level, String colorPrefix, String plainPrefix, String msg) {
    if (canLog(level)) {
        out.println((enableColor ? colorPrefix : plainPrefix) + msg);
    }
}
private static void logThrowable(Level level, Throwable t) {
    if (canLog(level)) {
        t.printStackTrace(out);
    }
}
```

I deliberately **left the `(String format, Object...)` variants unchanged** — their explicit `canLog` check skips the `format(...)` call when the level is disabled (a real optimization), so collapsing them would either lose that or double-check. The refactoring is behavior-identical by inspection: same guard, same prefix selection, same `out.println`/`printStackTrace` on the same (volatile) `out`.

